# Adding a New Card Issuer

## Step 1: Refactor category-mapper.ts (one-time, if still single-issuer)

The file currently has a single flat `DISCOVER_CATEGORY_MAP`. Before adding a second issuer, restructure to per-issuer dispatch:

```typescript
// Before (single-issuer)
const DISCOVER_CATEGORY_MAP: Record<string, Category | null> = { ... };
function mapIssuerCategory(sourceCategory) { ... }

// After (multi-issuer)
const ISSUER_CATEGORY_MAPS: Partial<Record<CardType, Record<string, Category | null>>> = {
  discover_it_student: { Restaurants: "Dining", ... },
  chase_freedom: { "Food & Drink": "Dining", ... },
};

function mapIssuerCategory(cardType: CardType, sourceCategory: string | null) {
  const map = ISSUER_CATEGORY_MAPS[cardType];
  if (!map || !sourceCategory) return null;
  const mapped = map[sourceCategory.trim()];
  ...
}
```

This changes the function signature — update all callers (categorizer.ts, extraction-pipeline.ts) to pass `cardType`.

## Step 2: Add the new CardType

- Add the new card type to the `CardType` union in `types/index.ts`
- Add a label in `CARD_LABELS`
- Add detection logic in `lib/services/card-detector.ts`

## Step 3: Add CSV/XLS parser support (if structured)

If the new issuer provides CSV/XLS, update `lib/parsers/csv-row-parser.ts`:
- Add header detection for the new format
- Map columns to `StructuredRow` fields (date, description, amount, category)

## Step 4: Populate the issuer category map

Map the new issuer's category strings to our `Category` values. Set ambiguous ones to `null` so they fall through to regex/AI tiers. Check the issuer's actual statement for the exact category strings they use.

## Key constraints

- Override map (category_overrides) is issuer-agnostic — learnings are shared across all cards
- Regex merchant rules in categorizer.ts are also issuer-agnostic — no changes needed there
- Never add a category string to `CATEGORIES` without updating all downstream consumers
